module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.CoherenceClosure
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Coherent sheaves of modules on a site

Stacks 01BV defines coherence for a sheaf of modules on a *ringed space*, and
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Coherent` formalizes it there. Every proof in
Stacks Section 17.21 that a sheaf of modules is coherent — tensor products (01CE), tensor,
exterior and symmetric powers (01CK), internal homs (01CQ) — produces instead a *local*
description of the sheaf, valid on the members of some open covering, and the sheaf so described
lives on a slice site `Over U`, which is not the site of opens of a space. So the closure theory
of coherent sheaves had no home for the slice-local step of any of those proofs.

This file removes that obstruction by defining coherence over an arbitrary site and re-deriving
the closure theory there. The slice-local step then becomes free: "`M` is coherent on `W`" is
`IsCoherent (M.over W)`, a statement of exactly the same form as coherence of `M` itself, and
`isCoherent_of_coversTop` glues such statements over a covering family.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.IsCoherent` is coherence of a sheaf of modules on an arbitrary
  site: the sheaf is of finite type, and for every object `U` of the site the kernel of every
  map from a finite free module into the restriction of the sheaf to `U` is of finite type.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.coherentModules` is coherence packaged as an `ObjectProperty`, and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.CoherentModules` is the corresponding full subcategory.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.overOverEquivalence` and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.overOverIso` are the slice-of-a-slice comparison. They are the
  universe-polymorphic forms of the constructions of the same names in
  `StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.CoherentWeakSerre`; see the implementation
  notes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isFiniteType_over_of_isFiniteType`: being of finite type is
  preserved by restriction to an object of the site. (That it is local on the base is already
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalKernel.isFiniteType_of_coversTop`, at these universes; see the
  implementation notes.)
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isFiniteType_X₂_of_shortExact`: **Stacks 01B7, second assertion,
  over an arbitrary site.** The space-level proof shrinks a neighbourhood of a point; over a
  site the sequence is instead restricted to a covering family on whose members the quotient is
  generated by finitely many sections, where
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isFiniteType_X₂_of_generatingSections` applies.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_of_mono` (**01BY (1)**),
  `isFiniteType_kernel_of_isCoherent` (**01BY (2)**), `isCoherent_kernel` and
  `isCoherent_X₃_of_shortExact` (**01BY (3)**), `isCoherent_X₂_of_shortExact` (**01BY (4)**) and
  `coherentModules_isWeakSerreClass` (**01BY (5)**), all over an arbitrary site.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_sigma`: a finite direct sum of coherent sheaves of
  modules is coherent.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_over`: **coherence restricts to a slice site**, and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_of_coversTop`: **coherence is local on the base**,
  packaged as `isCoherent_iff_forall_isCoherent_over`. The form in which Section 17.21 uses the
  latter is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_of_coversTop_of_mono`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_opens_of_locallyCokernelFiniteCoproduct`: **a sheaf of
  modules that is locally the cokernel of a map between finite direct sums of copies of a
  coherent sheaf is coherent.** This is the step that ends the proofs of Stacks 01CE and 01CK.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherentOn_iff_isCoherent_over`: **`IsCoherentOn M W` of
  `CoherenceClosure` is `IsCoherent (M.over W)`**, and
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_iff_isCoherent_opens`: over the site of opens of a
  space the predicate here is the space-level predicate of `Coherent`. With
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site.isCoherent_opens_of_coversTop` a local coherence statement on the
  slice sites of an open covering yields coherence for the space-level predicate.

## Implementation notes

**Which universes are independent, and which are not.** The site is `C : Type u` with
`Category.{v} C`, the sheaf of rings is `R : Sheaf J RingCat.{t}` and the sheaves of modules are
`SheafOfModules.{t} R`: three independent universes, where `Coherent.lean` and
`CoherentWeakSerre.lean` have one. The module universe is *not* independent of the ring universe,
and that is forced rather than chosen: `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections`,
`SheafOfModules.LocalGeneratorsData` and `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` are declared in Mathlib
for `M : SheafOfModules.{u} R` with `R : Sheaf J RingCat.{u}`, so there is no notion of "finite
type" for a sheaf of modules whose sections live in a universe different from that of the
structure ring, and hence none of coherence either. Decoupling them is a change to Mathlib, not
to this file.

**Why the equi-universe theory does not suffice, and why it is not reused.** The auxiliary
site-level results of `Coherent.lean` and `CoherentWeakSerre.lean` are stated at
`C : Type u` with `Category.{u} C` and `RingCat.{u}`. That is closed under slicing — `Over Y` is
again `Type u` with `Category.{u}` — which is exactly why it serves `Opens X`. It is not closed
under *decoupling*: for `C : Type u` with `Category.{v} C` the slice `Over Y` is `Type (max u v)`
with `Category.{v}`, so `u` and `v` cannot be identified. The results of those two files that are
needed here are therefore restated at three universes:
`localGeneratorsDataPushforward`, `localGeneratorsDataPushforward_isFiniteType`,
`isFiniteType_pushforward`, `over_mono`, `over_isZero`, `isFiniteType_of_iso`,
`isFiniteType_of_generatingSections`, `zeroGeneratingSections`, `isFiniteType_of_isZero`,
`overKernelIso`, `isLimitKernelForkOver`, `isFiniteType_kernel_of_epi_comp`,
`overOverEquivalence`, `overOverIso`, `isFiniteType_of_overOverEquivalence_inverse`,
`isFiniteType_overOverEquivalence_inverse_obj`, `generatingSectionsOverOver`,
`isFiniteType_over_of_isFiniteType`, `exists_generatingSections_X₂` and
`isFiniteType_pullback_of_shortExact`. The proofs are those of the originals; only the universe
annotations differ. The dedup owed, once the two space-level coherence predicates are gone, is to
delete the equi-universe copies and keep these.

What is *not* restated is the part of the substrate that is already universe-polymorphic and is
used as it stands. `Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalKernel` of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.TensorExactnessSheafify` already carries
`over_epi`, `isFiniteType_free`, `bindLocalGeneratorsData` and `isFiniteType_of_coversTop` at
three independent universes — those are *second* copies of `Coherent.lean`'s equi-universe
versions, and this file adds no third: it uses `LocalKernel`'s.
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.FiniteTypeExtension` is used directly (in
particular `isFiniteType_of_epi`, which is preferred throughout over `Coherent.lean`'s primed
equi-universe copy), as are
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.FiniteTypeKernel` and the abelian-category helpers
`epi_kernelMap_of_epi`, `kernelCompIsoOfFactorsThrough`, `isLimitKernelForkPullbackFst` and
`isLimitKernelForkPullbackSnd` of `CoherentWeakSerre.lean`.

**The sheafification hypotheses are assumed, not derived.** Kernels and cokernels of sheaves of
modules are sheafifications, so stating condition (2) of coherence at all needs
`SheafOfModules.{t} (R.over U)` to be abelian, hence `HasSheafify (J.over U) AddCommGrpCat.{t}`
and `(J.over U).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{t}`; and asking the resulting kernel to be
of finite type quantifies over the slice site of the slice site, hence the same instances one
level deeper. For an equi-universe site all of these are found automatically — the general
`HasSheafify` instance applies to `AddCommGrpCat.{max u v}` once
`Mathlib.Algebra.Category.Grp.FilteredColimits` is in the import closure, and that includes every
iterated slice, since equi-universe sites are closed under slicing. Once `t` is independent of
`u` and `v` they must be assumed, which is why they appear as instance arguments. This is a
faithful reading of the source, which assumes throughout that a site admits sheafification.
The proofs reach three slice levels deep, so the deepest hypothesis assumed is for
`((J.over Y).over Z).over W`; nothing here needs a fourth.

**Where the space is still used.** Nothing in this file needs a topological space. Two results of
the space-level theory are *not* generalized here, for the same reason: they are genuinely about
points. `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isFiniteType_middle_of_shortExact` (01B7 over a space) is replaced
by `isFiniteType_X₂_of_shortExact` above, which is proved by gluing rather than by shrinking a
neighbourhood. `Stacks.SheafOfModules.isCoherent_of_locallyKernelFiniteCoproduct` of
`CoherenceClosure.lean` takes a pointwise hypothesis and stays where it is; its content over a
site is `isCoherent_of_coversTop_of_mono` together with `isCoherent_sigma`.

**Three declarations of one notion, and what this file does about it.** Coherence of a sheaf of
modules over a ringed space is declared twice in this library as two distinct constants with
identical fields: `Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsCoherent` in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Coherent`, which the closure theory uses, and
`SheafOfModules.IsCoherent` in `StacksDraft.Ch17.Sec12.Tag01BW`, which
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Modules.Coherent` tags with 01BV. The predicate here is intended to
supersede **both**: each is its specialization to `J = Opens.grothendieckTopology X`, and
`isCoherent_iff_isCoherent_opens` proves that for the first. The bridge to the second is
deliberately *not* stated here, because `Tag01BW` carries an unrelated forty-module import list
(number theory, analysis, combinatorics) that has no business in a foundation module; it is the
two-line `fun h => ⟨h.1, h.2⟩` in either direction, and the right resolution is to delete that
copy rather than to bridge it. No `@[stacks]` attribute is carried here for the same reason that
there is no third predicate: 01BV and the parts of 01BY are already tagged on the space-level
declarations, and the tags should move onto these declarations when those are deleted — onto
`IsCoherent` and `CoherentModules` for
01BV, and onto `isCoherent_of_mono`, `isFiniteType_kernel_of_isCoherent`, `isCoherent_kernel`,
`isCoherent_X₃_of_shortExact`, `isCoherent_X₂_of_shortExact` and
`coherentModules_isWeakSerreClass` for the five parts of 01BY.

**Passing `(R := ...)` at a slice site.** The universe parameters of `IsCoherent` sit in front of
its instance arguments, so an application whose expected type is not yet known — `isCoherent_sigma`
or `isCoherent_cokernel` used inside a proof about a slice site — has its universe metavariables
defaulted to `0` before the argument that would determine them is elaborated, and fails. Supplying
the sheaf of rings, as in `isCoherent_cokernel (R := R.over U) f`, fixes all of them and is the
idiom used here. For the same reason `coherentModules_isClosedUnderCokernels` and its companions
are not usable by instance search at a site where the three universes coincide, since that asks
instance synthesis to solve `max t u =?= u`; `isCoherent_cokernel` and `isCoherent_of_iso` are
stated as theorems so that there is always a form that works. The five
`coherentModules_isClosedUnder…` instances here therefore duplicate the identically-worded ones
of `CoherenceClosure` and `CoherentWeakSerre` on purpose: those are stated at the module
universe of a ringed space, and routing this file at them leaves
`coherentModules_isWeakSerreClass` without its kernel, cokernel and extension fields.

**Why the coproduct trio cannot be folded the other way.** The three declarations
`coherentModules_isClosedUnderBinaryCoproducts`,
`coherentModules_isClosedUnderFiniteCoproducts` and `isCoherent_sigma` below have
docstrings and proof bodies identical to
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.coherentModules_isClosedUnderBinaryCoproducts`,
`coherentModules_isClosedUnderFiniteCoproducts` and `isCoherent_sigma` of `CoherenceClosure.lean`,
and the section below does quantify over `{J} {R}`, so the space-level trio looks like the
instantiation `J := Opens.grothendieckTopology X`. Deleting it in favour of the general forms was
tried and does not work, for three reasons, in increasing order of finality.

The instantiation is not an instantiation of the *statements*. `IsCoherent` here and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.IsCoherent` are distinct constants, hence `coherentModules R` here and
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.coherentModules R` are distinct `ObjectProperty` values, and the general
statement at `J := Opens.grothendieckTopology X` is about the former where the space-level one is
about the latter. The space-level statements do follow, but through
`isCoherent_iff_isCoherent_opens` below — for the two instances only after `funext` and `propext`
turn that iff into an equality of properties.

A proof that rewrites along a `propext` equality is a term, not an instance, and instance search
cannot reach the general instances at the site of opens in any case: by the previous paragraph
that asks it to solve `max t u =?= u`, and indeed
`(coherentModules R).IsClosedUnderBinaryCoproducts` here is not synthesizable at a sheaf of rings
on `Opens.grothendieckTopology X` while `Stacks.SheafOfModules`'s is. The space-level instances
are what makes `(Stacks.SheafOfModules.coherentModules R).prop_coproduct` elaborate, which is how
`CoherenceClosure.lean`'s `isCoherent_sigma` is proved.

Decisively, this file imports `CoherenceClosure.lean`, so the general forms are *downstream* of
the space-level ones, and `CoherenceClosure.lean`'s own
`isCoherent_of_locallyKernelFiniteCoproduct` uses its `isCoherent_sigma`. Routing that file here
is an import cycle. The trio stays duplicated until the two space-level predicates are folded
into this one — the dedup the note above already records as owed.

**The declarations live in the `Stacks.SheafOfModules.Site` namespace** rather than in
`Stacks.SheafOfModules`, so that this file can be co-imported with `Coherent.lean`, whose
`IsCoherent` would otherwise collide. After the dedup the namespace can be flattened by a
textual rename.

